# Pins a diagnostic ORDER, not just a diagnostic (docs/panel/065, condition 4).
# `i64[2]` and `i64[2][3]` are two type nodes sharing span.start, so the stable
# span sort in types/mod.rs cannot decide which prints first — the map's
# ascending-node-id order does (the `// ORDER:` mark in types/ffi_decls.rs), and
# it puts the inner array's diagnostic first. A port that sorts offenders by
# span alone swaps the pair (the compiler-engineer's prediction to score at
# M-selfhost-port).
#
# **The pair is told once since defect 405** (2026-10-06): the inner `i64[2]`
# is written inside the outer and is one mistake with it, so the sweep tells
# the outer `i64[2][3]` alone, whose `[T]` is the whole type's. The tie this
# case was written for no longer arises; what it pins now is that one
# message before `fixed_array_length` on the same line. The `ORDER:` key
# stands in `selfhost/check/ffi_sweep.hero`, the order the sweep pushes in.
#
# **And `fixed_array_length` left the line with defect 423** (the same day):
# the literal was judged against the `i64[2][3]` the sweep refuses, and the
# `[[i64]]` the refusal offers takes it, so that judgement is withdrawn
# (`selfhost/check/fixed_judged.hero`). The line holds one message, and
# `bad_operand` below is a use of the binding, which keeps what it was told.
function main()
    x: i64[2][3] @ [[1, 2, 3], [1, 2, 3]]  #~ fixed_outside_a_group
    print(len(x))  #~ bad_operand
